<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kpalimé</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Palime</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kpalimé is a town in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, in hilly country near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. The German colonial railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lomé</a> reached it in 1907 and became known as the cocoa railway because it carried cocoa and other produce from the region to the coast. It remains a market and agricultural centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kpalime.jpg" alt="Kpalimé" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HpBob · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kpalime.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kpalimé is a city in the Plateaux Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, 120 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lomé</a> and 15 km from the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. It is the administrative capital of Kloto Prefecture and has a population of 75,084, making it the fourth-biggest town in Togo after Lomé, Sokodé and Kara. It lies near Mount Agou, the highest point in Togo, and Mount Kloto, and is a base for visiting waterfalls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa plantations and the Misahöhe Forest Reserve. Ewe is the main local language and French, the official language of Togo, is spoken by anyone who has been to school.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kpalimé was originally Agomé-Kpalimé, one of the villages of the Agomé people, whose origins can probably be traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a>. According to the account of the town's origins, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agokoli">Agokoli</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notsie">Notsé</a>, a tyrant of the early 18th century, caused many people to flee, and groups of fugitives including the Agomé reached Mount Kloto. After a quarrel over a goat's head, the elders spread to the plain. The fifth group moved to Mokpalipé, meaning crossroads, a name that evolved into Kpalimé. The Agomé-Kpalimé later learned trade from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> traders who crossed their territory with caravans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salaga">Salaga</a> on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keta">Keta</a>. Its chiefs belong to the Apeto dynasty, though the paramount chief of the Agomé is the chief of Agomé-Yo.</p><p>In 1890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesko-von-puttkamer">Jesko von Puttkamer</a>, German Imperial Commissioner for Togoland, founded a field station at Misahohè, 9 km away. Kpalimé soon overtook it as regional capital, and by 1913 had 39 businesses. The Germans opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lomé</a> to Kpalimé branch of Togo Railways in 1907 and built the neo-Gothic Cathedral of the Holy Spirit in 1913. British forces moving in from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> occupied the town from 1914 to 1920, after which it was ceded to the French following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. It was thus one of the few places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to pass from German to British to French administration. Under the French its status rose from indigenous commune in 1939 to mixed commune in 1951 and commune in 1959.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kpalimé is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>'s main centre for crafts such as wood sculpture, weaving, wickerwork, batiks and pottery, with 36 artisanal workshops and retail outlets and an artisanal training college. It is the seat of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Kpalimé, established in 1994. The Misahöhe Forest Reserve, covering 5,000 hectares north-west of the town, is the only site in Togo from which many forest bird species have been recorded.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kpalimé” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They built a wharf at Lome and three railways, including the line to <b>Palime</b> known as the cocoa railway, and promoted cotton, cocoa and palm products.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-togoland">German Togoland</a></li></ul></section>
